This is not a fix

Once again, sorry for the delay. And thanks to @ArminShoeibi, @julealgon, @KalleOlaviNiemitalo and @pinkfloydx33 for stepping in (here and in the PR) and sharing links to docs and issues.

@stormaref I can see that you feel strongly about it, and I understand your frustration. I agree it's one of these nasty things that catch you by surprise. But while it's disappointing -- this is NOT a bug.

A bug is something which isn't working _as intended_. But Typed clients were designed and implemented this way _from the very beginning_. And this behavior is documented in several places. Including the workarounds to use if the default behavior doesn't suit your needs.

Most importantly, it is [documented](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.extensions.dependencyinjection.httpclientfactoryservicecollectionextensions.addhttpclient?view=net-9.0-pp#microsoft-extensions-dependencyinjection-httpclientfactoryservicecollectionextensions-addhttpclient-2(microsoft-extensions-dependencyinjection-iservicecollection-system-action((system-iserviceprovider-system-net-http-httpclient)))) that
  1. a Typed client is a transient service linked to _a Named client_ 
      - `AddHttpClient<TClient,TImplementation>: ... configures a binding between the TClient type and a named HttpClient.`
      - `TClient: The type specified will be registered in the service collection as a transient service.`
  2. all configuration is _forwarded_ to the _underlying Named client_
      - `configureClient: A delegate that is used to configure an HttpClient.`
      - `HttpClient instances that apply the provided configuration can be retrieved using CreateClient(String) and providing the matching name.`
  3. a name of this Named client is either _specified_, or if it will be set to the _type name of TClient_.
      - `The client name will be set to the type name of TClient.`

By the way, this has another "interesting" (read: surprising in a bad way) consequence. Beyond the fact that the name comes from the _interface_ type (`TClient`), instead of the _implementation_ type  (`TImplementation`)... the name also _does NOT include the namespace_. Which means you can't have two Typed clients like e.g. `MyApp.IService` and `Common.IService` -- without explicitly specifying the names. 🙃

Honestly, the design of Typed clients... was taking it too far. While it meant to simplify the usage, it ended up hiding and obscuring way too much under the hood -- giving you _false impression_ of a "plain straightforward DI injection". It is NOT. Those Named `HttpClient` instances are not part of the DI container. Typed Clients are INHERENTLY "inconsistent with the expected functionality of dependency injection", as you put it. Because the expected functionality should put the SAME implementation in all the services that have an `HttpClient` in their constructors, shouldn't it?

Anyway. We can argue whether the past design decisions were good or not -- and we can even agree that some weren't. But reality is, we still have to live with all of them. There is a very high bar for introducing any kind of breaking changes. And changing the _default way_ the Typed clients are linked to Named clients, that was there many years _since the beginning_, and especially given that it ships out-of-band as a .NET Standard package... imagine having to update a package (maybe even accidentally through transitive dependencies) in some long existing, legacy .NET Framework app... not touching the code, and everything even compiles nicely -- but randomly something starts misbehaving at runtime? Because someone long time ago decided to depend on that contract... Yeah. _[UPD: shared a couple of [specific examples](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/110999#issuecomment-2594275382) that would break.]_ In a by-design cases like this, sometimes the best one can do is to highlight the confusing behavior in docs.

This doesn't mean we don't strive to improve stuff. We do, more than anything. In .NET 8, Keyed DI was introduced, and in .NET 9, it was integrated with HttpClientFactory. I'd say, Keyed DI + Named clients should give you the exact consistency and straightforwardness that Typed Clients were lacking. Your code would change only slightly:


```c#
public class TypedClientA : ITypedClient
{
    public TypedClientA([FromKeyedServices("A")] HttpClient httpClient)
    //{ ...
}

public class TypedClientB : ITypedClient
{
    public TypedClientB([FromKeyedServices("B")] HttpClient httpClient)
    //{ ...
}

// ...

builder.Services.AddTransient<ITypedClient, TypedClientA>();
builder.Services.AddTransient<ITypedClient, TypedClientB>();

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("A", h => h.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://exampleA.com")).AddAsKeyed();
builder.Services.AddHttpClient("B", h => h.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://exampleB.com")).AddAsKeyed();

// ...
//public SomeController(IEnumerable<ITypedClient> typedClients) -- stays the same
```

It was not to give you a "yet another workaround". It's more: "maybe Typed clients are fundamentally broken, but now there is a way that's not".

Sorry for branching a bit. The bottom line is:

------

Triage:
This is not a bug. It is expected, documented behavior. And a workaround exist, so the issue is not blocking. 
A proposed change will be breaking, and there's not enough evidence to meet the bar.

I will close both the issue and the PR as "Won't Fix". Please let me know if I missed something. Thanks!
